# Plans

A Plan is Main's durable cross-session memory for one substantive user goal. Native host task state and chat history are temporary; the active Markdown Plan records what must survive compaction, a new session, or a new Main Agent.

Use `exec-plans/_template.md` with `format: 2` and `task_graph: 1`. Preserve those fields and the example requirements when creating or resuming a Plan.

## When to use a Plan

Create or resume one active Plan for cross-cutting, risky, long-running, multi-agent, public-contract, persistent-data, security, infrastructure, deployment, or otherwise substantive work. Small local reversible changes may proceed without one.

Do not create a second task database, packet directory, lease, journal, scheduler, or orchestration layer.

## Ownership and lifecycle

- Main alone serializes Plan edits and owns goal, scope, non-goals, acceptance, decomposition, dispatch, integration, decisions, checkpoint, exact next action, verification judgment, and terminal transition. Main serializes a finite accepted manifest before resuming an Owner.
- Sub Agents receive one bounded Task Packet and return Plan-ready evidence; they do not concurrently edit the Plan.
- Broad or multi-part roots default to `Role: Task Owner` and `May delegate: yes`; narrow or inherently serial roots remain direct nondelegating leaves.
- One substantive goal maps to one file in `exec-plans/active/`.
- Terminal Plans move to `exec-plans/completed/` with actual status `complete`, `cancelled`, or `superseded`.
- Historical Plans remain readable. Supersede or annotate history; do not rewrite old results to match a new process.

## First-Plan handoff

Main—not package runtime—creates and resumes Plans. Before dispatching a substantive goal, Main searches both `exec-plans/active/` and `exec-plans/completed/` for ordinary Markdown Plans matching the goal. Resume exactly one matching active Plan; stop if multiple active Plans match. Completed Plans are historical and are searched to prevent ID reuse, not resumed.

Otherwise choose the lowest unused current-year numeric ID across both directories, copy `_template.md` (`exec-plans/_template.md`) to a new file in `exec-plans/active/`, and never overwrite/reuse an ID. Keep state in visible Markdown. Never use a Plan CLI/hidden state/automatic dispatcher/closure. Main serializes a finite accepted manifest before resuming an Owner.

## Dynamic procedure Skills

Only complete Confirmed structured procedure records can produce a project-owned instruction-only `.claude/skills/<slug>/SKILL.md` through resumed `reporivet setup`. Do not pre-generate a project-specific Skill. Keep its frontmatter instruction-only and least privilege; no procedure is inferred or executed, and existing differing, stale, or arbitrary Skills are preserved rather than deleted.

## Broad-milestone native-Agent dispatch

For every milestone classified as broad, this is a common installed-project rule:

`T<n> (broad milestone) -> T<n>-A/B/C/... (owned child packets, all ready leaves dispatched concurrently) -> T<n>-I (integration) -> T<n>-V1/V2/... (parallel fresh verification)`

Main dispatches independent root Owners concurrently using only the host's native Agent execution. A resumed Owner dispatches its complete dependency-ready descendants through host-native Agent execution and only within the finite child budget in the accepted manifest. Main dispatches the complete dependency-ready root set concurrently: independent Owners for broad work and direct leaves for narrow or inherently serial work. This rule does not apply to inherently single or serial milestones.

Every child row retains an explicit owner and matching bounded packet. Owner boundaries, child budget, disjoint allowed-write sets, exact baseline, frozen shared interfaces, and separate worktrees remain explicit. Descendants inherit parent scope, protected paths, acceptance, exact baseline, and frozen shared interfaces and cannot broaden them. An Owner performs local aggregation of descendant results and returns one Plan-ready evidence package to Main; Main alone performs final integration.

If a child is itself broad, only a packet explicitly marked `Role: Task Owner` and `May delegate: yes` may run its predeclared bounded descendant packets such as `T<n>-A-1`; ordinary leaf Agents do not delegate. Existing narrow or inherently serial packets remain compatible as direct nondelegating leaves and may omit hierarchy-only fields.

Parallel mutable siblings require disjoint allowed-write sets, frozen shared interfaces, an exact common baseline, and separate worktrees. Read-only review and verification lanes may run concurrently. Fresh verification is read-only, nonrepairing, and nondelegating; verification nodes depend on the integrated candidate.

Reporivet installs no scheduler, task store, lease, lock, or automatic dispatcher; it also installs no dispatcher, runtime, hidden state, task DB, runner, generated CI, Gate, evidence archive, deployment engine, or automatic closure.

## Minimum active Plan content

Use `exec-plans/_template.md` with `format: 2` and `task_graph: 1`. Keep it concise but restartable:

- frontmatter retaining identity, lifecycle status, `format: 2`, `task_graph: 1`, and candidate hashes;
- original goal and observable acceptance criteria;
- scope and non-goals;
- task rows retaining Owner, State, Depends on, Outcome, Result, and an optional Parallel group;
- decisions and discoveries;
- current checkpoint and exact next action;
- integration and verification summaries, candidate identity, and residual risks.

A Task Packet names outcome, non-goals, exact reads, allowed writes, protected paths, acceptance criteria, verification commands, stop conditions, and compact return evidence. Broad-milestone child packets also name Role, Parent, Parallel group, May delegate, inherited boundaries, finite child budget, exact baseline, disjoint write isolation, and Return; an inherently single or serial milestone may omit hierarchy-only fields. Task Owners locally aggregate descendant results and do not edit the shared Plan.

## Completion

Completion requires observable acceptance, integrated project-owned verification, independent review when required, current-state documentation parity, recorded follow-ups, and Main or human evidence judgment. No repository-local completion engine, model judge, automatic closure command, or evidence archive decides completion. Reporivet installs no scheduler, dispatcher, runtime, hidden state, or automatic closure.
